  "slug": "quantum-classical-636a",
  "summary": "Five preprints tighten digitization and Trotter error control and probe classical simulability, sharpening resource estimates for Hamiltonian simulation.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is quantifying and reducing approximation error in Hamiltonian simulation. For U(1) lattice gauge theory, a semiclassical analysis gives an improved digitization error bound for low-lying eigenstates, sharpening qubit resource estimates. For Coulomb dynamics, Trotter and qDRIFT errors decay slowly because of the singularity; supersmoothing qDRIFT is introduced, with error linear in time. A phase-operator estimate of Trotter error, expressed through nested commutators, is reported as tighter than prior bounds for finite-size systems. Another paper finds Trotter errors in observables can be exponentially smaller for state 2-designs than 1-designs, quantified via frame potential. A classical free-probability algorithm for sparse random non-local Hamiltonians computes spectral fluctuations, thermal values and correlation functions, delimiting the quantum-classical boundary.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-27"
}
